For the hypothetical `foo()` function that I'd like to deprcate, I'll write a deprecation notice that gives users a clear idea of what changed, how to migrate their code, and a timeline for when the function will be fully removed.

```typescript
/**
 * @deprecated Replaced by [AlternateFunction] in v2.0.0. This function will be removed in v3.0.0.
 * The `foo()` function has been replaced with [AlternateFunction] to provide better performance and usability.
 * Use the new function to get the same results.
 */
export function foo(): never {
  // do something that nobody should be calling
}
```

In addition to the deprecation notice itself, it's a good practice to keep track of which versions implement the deprecation notice and which versions remove the deprecated function. Those details are important for migrating code on a timely basis and preventing surprises during maintenance windows.
